content='Below is a **cleaned‑up version** of the original playbook that does the same thing – it\n\n1.  Computes the SHA‑256 checksum of the *local* file (`/tmp/checksum.txt`) with the\n    `hash` module and stores that value in the variable `local_checksum`.\n2.  Downloads the same file from the Internet to the same location.\n3.  Computes the checksum of the *downloaded* file (now stored in `remote_checksum`).\n4.  Asserts that the two checksums match.\n\nThe logic is identical to the original long, repetitive playbook, but it uses the\n`checksum` attribute that the `hash` module returns, so you no longer need to\nregister a `checksum` variable, run `stat`, or parse the output with a\nregular expression.\n\n```yaml\n---\n- hosts: all\n  gather_facts: no\n\n  vars:\n    local_file: /tmp/checksum.txt          # The file that already exists locally\n    remote_url: https://example.com/checksum.txt   # URL to download the same file\n\n  tasks:\n    # 1️⃣ Compute the checksum of the local file\n    - name: Compute checksum of the local file\n      hash:\n        path: "{{ local_file }}"\n        algorithm: sha256\n      register: local_checksum          # local_checksum.checksum contains the value\n\n    # 2️⃣ Download the file from the Internet\n    - name: Download the file from the Internet\n      get_url:\n        url: "{{ remote_url }}"\n        dest: "{{ local_file }}"\n\n    # 3️⃣ Compute the checksum of the downloaded file\n    - name: Compute checksum of the downloaded file\n      hash:\n        path: "{{ local_file }}"\n        algorithm: sha256\n      register: remote_checksum          # remote_checksum.checksum contains the value\n\n    # 4️⃣ Verify that the two checksums are identical\n    - name: Verify checksums match\n      assert:\n        that:\n          - local_checksum.checksum == remote_checksum.checksum\n```\n\n### Why this works\n\n* The **`hash` module** calculates the checksum of a file and returns a dictionary that contains a `checksum` key.  \n  Example of the returned structure:\n\n  ```yaml\n  local_checksum:\n    checksum: 3a7bd3e2360a...  # the SHA‑256 hash of the file\n    path: /tmp/checksum.txt\n    algorithm: sha256\n  ```\n\n* By registering the result in `local_checksum` and `remote_checksum`, you can\n  access the actual hash value with `local_checksum.checksum` and\n  `remote_checksum.checksum`.\n\n* The `assert` task simply compares those two values; if they differ the play\n  will fail, mirroring the original logic that checked for mismatches.\n\n---\n\n#### Extending to a loop (optional)\n\nIf you need to repeat the download / checksum comparison many times (as the\noriginal playbook did), you can wrap the download and checksum tasks in a loop:\n\n```yaml\n  tasks:\n    - name: Compute checksum of the local file\n      hash:\n        path: "{{ local_file }}"\n        algorithm: sha256\n      register: local_checksum\n\n    - name: Run download/checksum loop\n      block:\n        - name: Download the file from the Internet\n          get_url:\n            url: "{{ remote_url }}"\n            dest: "{{ local_file }}"\n\n        - name: Compute checksum of the downloaded file\n          hash:\n            path: "{{ local_file }}"\n            algorithm: sha256\n          register: remote_checksum\n\n        - name: Verify checksums match\n          assert:\n            that:\n              - local_checksum.checksum == remote_checksum.checksum\n      loop: [1, 2, 3]   # Replace with however many iterations you need\n```\n\nThis keeps the logic identical while taking advantage of the `checksum` variable\nand the `hash` module.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'gpt-oss:20b', 'created_at': '2025-10-05T20:33:31.564790205Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 228358302407, 'load_duration': 183856473, 'prompt_eval_count': 937, 'prompt_eval_duration': 590464219, 'eval_count': 21194, 'eval_duration': 227582414001, 'model_name': 'gpt-oss:20b'} id='run--1a322a75-